{"id": "2026-11-28-news-blocher-calls-svp-ticket-an-accident", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Blocher calls SVP ticket an accident", "summary": "Former Federal Councillor Christoph Blocher on Friday 27 November called the SVP's official two-man ticket for the 9 December Federal Council election a \"Betriebsunfall\" (an accident), and hinted that a candidate elected outside it might not be expelled from the party.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/bundesratswahlen-ausgerechnet-blocher-deutet-hintertuer-fuer-wilde-wahl-an", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "Former Federal Councillor Christoph Blocher on Friday 27 November called the SVP's official two-man ticket for the 9 December Federal Council election a \"Betriebsunfall\" (an accident), and hinted that a candidate elected outside it might not be expelled from the party. Speaking on his YouTube programme \"Teleblocher\", the 86-year-old said both nominees, Thomas Aeschi and Michael Graber, were \"capable and electable\", but regretted that neither comes from French-speaking Switzerland and said he would have preferred Bernese cantonal minister Pierre Alain Schnegg alongside Aeschi. SVP statutes bar anyone who accepts a Federal Council seat without the parliamentary group's nomination from party membership. Blocher said Schnegg could still be readmitted by a two-thirds vote of the party executive and the group, but ruled that out for Geneva National Councillor Céline Amaudruz. He did not call for a \"wild\" election, and warned other parties against one. That rule exists because of Blocher himself: parliament voted him out in 2007 in favour of Eveline Widmer-Schlumpf, who had not been on the SVP ticket. SVP National Councillor Michael Götte defended the group's secret-ballot nomination as \"a clean process\". SP and FDP members told SRF their positions were unchanged, and the Centre group said it would hear only the two official candidates.", "why": "if parliament keeps to the ticket, French-speaking Switzerland drops to one seat in the seven-member government when Vaud's Guy Parmelin leaves. Blocher's remarks give the parties looking for an alternative an opening twelve days before the vote.", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-blocher-calls-svp-ticket-an-accident", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["SVP", "Christoph Blocher", "Federal Council", "Pierre Alain Schnegg", "Thomas Aeschi"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-osce-agrees-2027-budget-under-swiss-chair", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "OSCE agrees 2027 budget under Swiss chair", "summary": "The 57 member states of the OSCE (Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe) agreed its 2027 budget on Friday 27 November, the second budget in a row under Switzerland's chairmanship after five years without one.", "url": "https://www.osce.org/secretariat/670236", "source_domain": "osce.org", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR", "new source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The 57 member states of the OSCE (Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe) agreed its 2027 budget on Friday 27 November, the second budget in a row under Switzerland's chairmanship after five years without one. The 2027 Unified Budget is €123 million and keeps the savings made in 2026, including a temporary salary freeze. Every participating state, Russia and Ukraine included, must agree to it. \"A functioning OSCE needs a budget. Without it, essential activities, such as election observation, can no longer be guaranteed,\" said Chairman-in-Office and Swiss Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis. The budget comes ahead of the OSCE ministerial council that Switzerland hosts in Lugano on 3–4 December, where the 57 states will discuss European security and the organisation's ability to act.", "why": "OSCE election monitoring and field missions in Ukraine's neighbourhood and the Balkans depend on this money. Holding a consensus budget together is the main concrete test of the Swiss chairmanship before it ends this year.", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-osce-agrees-2027-budget-under-swiss-chair", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["OSCE", "Ignazio Cassis", "Lugano"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-geneva-to-prescribe-cocaine-to-crack-users", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Geneva to prescribe cocaine to crack users", "summary": "Geneva will next year start prescribing cocaine, smoked in a supervised inhalation room, to 10 to 20 people severely dependent on crack, Le Temps reported on Friday 27 November.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/suisse/geneve/pour-lutter-contre-le-crack-geneve-se-lance-dans-la-prescription-de-cocaine-une-premiere-mondiale", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Geneva will next year start prescribing cocaine, smoked in a supervised inhalation room, to 10 to 20 people severely dependent on crack, Le Temps reported on Friday 27 November. The pilot runs at CAAP Arve, an addiction unit of Geneva University Hospitals (HUG). It has been approved by the Federal Office of Public Health and endorsed by the cantonal government. Participation is voluntary and follows a detailed assessment. The scheme will be combined with psychotherapy, a possible treatment with long-acting stimulants, and neuromodulation, meaning treatments that alter nerve activity, for example with magnetic fields. Its promoters call it \"a world first\".", "why": "it applies to crack the model behind Switzerland's heroin-assisted treatment of the 1990s, in which doctors prescribe the drug itself to severely dependent users under supervision.", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-geneva-to-prescribe-cocaine-to-crack-users", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Geneva", "HUG", "FOPH"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-us-disables-cargo-ship-in-gulf-of-oman", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "US disables cargo ship in Gulf of Oman", "summary": "A US aircraft fired on the Palau-flagged cargo ship Pacific Archer in the Gulf of Oman on Friday 27 November and disabled its propulsion, according to US Central Command (CENTCOM), which said the ship had broken the US blockade of Iranian ports.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/us-disables-ship-in-gulf-of-oman-under-iran-blockade/a-80082950", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A US aircraft fired on the Palau-flagged cargo ship Pacific Archer in the Gulf of Oman on Friday 27 November and disabled its propulsion, according to US Central Command (CENTCOM), which said the ship had broken the US blockade of Iranian ports. CENTCOM said the aircraft hit the ship's stern with \"precision munitions\" after it left the Iranian port of Bandar Abbas, and that the civilian crew was unharmed. Tagesschau reports that ship-tracking sites gave Singapore as its destination, and that CENTCOM said the ship ignored several warnings. CENTCOM says the blockade has now disabled six ships and turned back 158 others. Washington imposed it in mid-July after a deal with Tehran collapsed. Iran has largely closed the Strait of Hormuz since the US and Israel began the war on Iran at the end of February. Separately, Kuwait summoned Iran's ambassador over Thursday's drone strike that killed two Italian technicians, and said it reserved the right to self-defence.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-us-disables-cargo-ship-in-gulf-of-oman", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Iran", "CENTCOM", "Gulf of Oman", "Pacific Archer"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-israeli-strike-kills-two-msf-watchmen-in-gaza", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Israeli strike kills two MSF watchmen in Gaza", "summary": "An Israeli strike on a Doctors Without Borders (MSF) clinic in Gaza City on Friday 27 November killed two of the clinic's watchmen.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/moyen-orient/20261128-deux-employ%C3%A9s-de-m%C3%A9decins-sans-fronti%C3%A8res-tu%C3%A9s-par-une-frappe-isra%C3%A9lienne-%C3%A0-gaza", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "An Israeli strike on a Doctors Without Borders (MSF) clinic in Gaza City on Friday 27 November killed two of the clinic's watchmen. MSF called it a \"direct hit\"; the Israeli army said both men were Hamas members. MSF said the premises were clearly marked and that their location had been given to the Israeli military. It said the deaths bring to 17 the number of its staff killed in Gaza. On Telegram, the Israeli army named the men and said one commanded a Hamas artillery unit; it has not published evidence for this. WHO chief Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus called the attack \"outrageous and unacceptable\". Earlier this week a strike killed Adel al-Helou, a UNRWA (UN Palestinian refugee agency) worker of 35 years, while he was supplying water in Jabalia camp. Israeli strikes have continued despite the ceasefire in force since October 2025.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-israeli-strike-kills-two-msf-watchmen-in-gaza", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Israel", "Gaza", "MSF", "Hamas"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-canada-s-top-court-upholds-quebec-s-bill-21", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Canada's top court upholds Quebec's Bill 21", "summary": "Canada's Supreme Court on Friday 27 November upheld almost all of Quebec's 2019 secularism law, Bill 21, which bars some public servants, such as teachers and police officers, from wearing religious symbols at work.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/canada-supreme-court-largely-upholds-quebec-secularism-bill/a-80082571", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Canada's Supreme Court on Friday 27 November upheld almost all of Quebec's 2019 secularism law, Bill 21, which bars some public servants, such as teachers and police officers, from wearing religious symbols at work. The court struck down only the provision covering members of Quebec's National Assembly, who may keep wearing them, as Quebec courts had already ruled. Chief Justice Richard Wagner wrote that the law \"is rooted in Quebec's legitimate and historical commitment\" to state secularism. Quebec passed it using the constitution's \"notwithstanding clause\", which lets a province shield a law from parts of the Charter of Rights, including freedom of religion. That made the case a test of how far provinces can use the clause. Critics say the law falls hardest on Muslim women who wear the hijab, and rights groups across Canada warned the ruling widens what provincial governments can do.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-canada-s-top-court-upholds-quebec-s-bill-21", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Supreme Court of Canada", "Quebec", "Bill 21"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-un-agencies-warn-of-16-hunger-hotspots", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "UN agencies warn of 16 hunger hotspots", "summary": "Hunger will worsen in the next six months in 16 hotspots, the UN's Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and World Food Programme (WFP) warned in their joint Hunger Hotspots report on Friday 27 November.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/monde/20261128-l-onu-alerte-sur-une-aggravation-de-la-faim-dans-le-monde-en-raison-des-conflit-et-d-el-ni%C3%B1o", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["world", "geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Hunger will worsen in the next six months in 16 hotspots, the UN's Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and World Food Programme (WFP) warned in their joint Hunger Hotspots report on Friday 27 November. Four hotspots are new this year, three of them driven by an unusually strong El Niño, the Pacific warming that shifts rainfall worldwide. By March, millions in Zimbabwe, Mozambique and Malawi face high levels of acute food insecurity. Central America had its driest first growing season in 40 years, and about three-quarters of the harvest there was lost, according to RFI's account of the report. Conflict remains the main cause of hunger: the most critical areas are Sudan, South Sudan, Somalia, parts of Nigeria and Yemen, followed by Afghanistan, DR Congo, the Palestinian territories and Haiti. The agencies add that episodes of political violence are up more than 15% on a year earlier, while humanitarian aid has collapsed.", "why": "Switzerland is among the WFP's donors and hosts much of the humanitarian system in Geneva. The report is the agencies' case for funding just as donors' aid budgets shrink.", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-un-agencies-warn-of-16-hunger-hotspots", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-27", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["FAO", "WFP", "Sudan", "El Niño"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-taiwan-counts-local-votes-kmt-leads-taipei", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Taiwan counts local votes, KMT leads Taipei", "summary": "Taiwan voted in local elections on Saturday 28 November, and early counts showed Kuomintang (KMT) incumbents leading in Taipei and Taoyuan and the governing Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) ahead in Taichung, Tainan and Kaohsiung, as of 6 p.m.", "url": "https://focustaiwan.tw/politics/202611285002", "source_domain": "focustaiwan.tw", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Taiwan voted in local elections on Saturday 28 November, and early counts showed Kuomintang (KMT) incumbents leading in Taipei and Taoyuan and the governing Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) ahead in Taichung, Tainan and Kaohsiung, as of 6 p.m. Taipei time (11:00 in Zurich). These are TV-network tallies; the Central Election Commission's official count was coming in more slowly. About 11,000 posts in 22 cities and counties were contested. The most watched race was Taipei, where KMT mayor Chiang Wan-an led the DPP's Puma Shen by roughly 36,000–44,000 votes on partial counts. Shen is a lawmaker and researcher on China's information warfare whom Chinese police have declared wanted for \"separatism\". New Taipei, the most populous city, was too close to call. KMT candidates had declared victory in Keelung, Miaoli, Nantou, Taitung, Kinmen and Lienchiang. Voters also decided National Referendum No. 22, on abolishing the \"nuclear-free homeland\" energy policy. In the last local elections, in 2022, the KMT won 14 of the 22 races.", "why": "the vote is the main test of President Lai Ching-te's DPP before the 2028 presidential election, and Beijing is watching Shen's race closely.", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-taiwan-counts-local-votes-kmt-leads-taipei", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-28", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Taiwan", "KMT", "DPP", "Chiang Wan-an", "Puma Shen"]}
{"id": "2026-11-28-news-le-pen-aide-suspected-of-receiving-ministry-leak", "date": "2026-11-28", "stream": "news", "headline": "Le Pen aide suspected of receiving ministry leaks", "summary": "Marine Le Pen's former chief of staff, Ambroise de Rancourt, is suspected of receiving confidential interior-ministry information, a source close to the case told AFP on Friday 27 November, after investigators searched his office at National Rally (RN) headquarters and his home on 9 November.", "url": "https://www.letemps.ch/monde/france/une-perquisition-chez-l-ex-directeur-de-cabinet-de-marine-le-pen-qui-aurait-recu-des-informations-confidentielles", "source_domain": "letemps.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Marine Le Pen's former chief of staff, Ambroise de Rancourt, is suspected of receiving confidential interior-ministry information, a source close to the case told AFP on Friday 27 November, after investigators searched his office at National Rally (RN) headquarters and his home on 9 November. Police internal-affairs investigators (IGPN) carried out the searches with two Paris investigating judges. De Rancourt now coordinates Le Pen's presidential programme for the April 2027 election. According to AFP, the leaks allegedly came from Hugues Moutouh, then secretary-general of the interior ministry and now regional prefect of Centre-Val de Loire, and concerned investigations into the party. Prosecutors opened a preliminary inquiry into breaches of professional and investigative secrecy in July. Le Pen said she had \"never\" received confidential information and that the search \"does not at all implicate\" the party. Her own conviction for misusing EU funds, upheld on appeal, is suspended pending a ruling by France's highest court.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-28-news-le-pen-aide-suspected-of-receiving-ministry-leak", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-28", "event_date": "2026-11-09", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Marine Le Pen", "National Rally", "Ambroise de Rancourt", "France"]}
